('89710462-c694-597c-a720-630833ce1179'::uuid,'c906fb73-66a3-5477-8732-349b15f6e5c4'::uuid,'Consolidated Draft 1.0',E'# Chapter 1. Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability\n\n| **Chapter at a Glance** |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| What this chapter does  | Sets who decides what in DRRM: the Mayor, the Sangguniang Panlungsod, the CDRRMC, the CDRRMO and the activated EOC. It fixes the two declaration routes (State of Calamity under RA 10121; State of Imminent Disaster under RA 12287) and the records that prove each decision. |\n| Main procedures         | GOV-01 to GOV-10                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Read with               | Chapters 2, 4 and 6                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| Status                  | Draft 1.3, 12 November 2026. Working draft for validation (workshop 1–4 December 2026); not approved.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, governance and institutional accountability framework for the City’s CDRRMO and EOC. It defines how the City Government organises, directs, coordinates, monitors and improves its DRRM functions across normal operations, preparedness, anticipatory action, emergency response and recovery, and it sets the decision rights that every later chapter relies on.\n\nThis chapter opens directly with its own findings. The purpose of the Manual, the City context, the assessment method (IAT v1.7, evidence rule, maturity bands), the design principles, the systems-thinking and quality approach and the document conventions are set out once in the Introduction to the Manual.\n\n### 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities\n\nThe October 2026 assessment identifies a significant distinction between the maturity of the CDRRMO, the EOC and their institutional interface.\n\n**Figure 1.1**\n\n*Institutional Maturity and Mandatory Compliance, IAT v1.7*\n\n![Figure 6: 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note. Maturity index on a 0–4 scale. The Institutional Maturity Index (IMI) combines Parts A and B; Part C (institutional interface) is reported separately. Mandatory compliance is the share of scored mandatory indicators rated 3 or 4. Source: Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.7, Dashboard.*\n\nThe results demonstrate that the CDRRMO has a stronger institutional foundation than the EOC, while the interface between the two remains the weakest component. This is a critical systems issue: the existence of functioning CDRRMO activities does not automatically translate into a coordinated, documented and reliable EOC operating system.\n\nThe dashboard identifies 30 high-priority gaps in Parts A and B: 18 in the CDRRMO assessment (Part A) and 12 in the EOC assessment (Part B). The separate Part C assessment adds 2 high-priority interface gaps (32 in all) that need action by other City Government offices and external partners.\n\nFor this chapter, the most consequential findings are:\n\n**Table 1.1**\n\n*Most Consequential Assessment Findings for Governance (IAT v1.7)*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 52%" />\n<col style="width: 32%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Assessment Reference</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Finding (IAT v1.7)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Governance Implication</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th><p>A1.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></th>\n<th>Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014, as amended by No. 011, s. 2015, establishes the CDRRMO under the Office of the City Mayor with a Head and three LDRRMO I posts for the JMC 2014-1 functions. It does not make the office a regular department. The ordinance was not among the documents reviewed in October 2026.</th>\n<th>Amend Ordinance No. 008 to give the office department status and a fuller plantilla; update the A1.1 evidence at validation.</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>A1.2 – Score 3</th>\n<th>EO 2024-032A names all RA 10121 Sec. 11 seats, including five CSO and two private-sector seats. Gaps: the academe seat was dropped; the City College of Ormoc, recommended by CDRRMC Res. No. 10 s.2024, has no amending EO; SP accreditation of the CSOs is not shown; the third private-sector seat is vacant.</th>\n<th>Issue an amending EO and keep a yearly membership register.</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>A1.3 – Score 3</th>\n<th>Meetings and resolutions are on file for 2024–2025, but not for four consecutive quarters. The presiding officer changes from meeting to meeting without a written designation by the Chair.</th>\n<th>Keep quarterly meetings; file minutes and attendance each quarter; designate the presiding officer in writing.</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>A1.4 – Score 3</th>\n<th>The CDRRMO acts as Secretariat, but no written secretariat procedure exists. Records lapses: an undated resolution, a reused control number, missing certifications, and two 2022 resolutions filed under each other''s numbers.</th>\n<th>Formalise the Secretariat SOP and a resolution register (GOV-02).</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>A1.5 – Score 3</th>\n<th>Evacuation and class-suspension rules are applied event by event (CDRRMC Res. Nos. 08 and 09 s.2021 for Typhoon Odette; Res. No. 06 s.2022 for TD Agaton). The evacuation ordinance lacks procedural steps, and the Zoning Ordinance has no hazard overlay zones.</th>\n<th>Link ordinances to warning levels and risk information.</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><p>A1.6 – Score 3</p>\n<p>(Medium)</p></th>\n<th>The State of Calamity process has been used (SP Res. No. 2021-366, Odette; CDRRMC Res. No. 06 s.2024, dengue), but there is no stand-alone written protocol, and Quick Response Fund resolutions do not cite the declaration relied on.</th>\n<th>Standardise declaration, QRF use and lifting (GOV-04).</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>A1.7 – Score 3</th>\n<th>DRRM takes part in the LFC review and the CDP. No CDC or LFC minutes show CDRRMO input changing a decision.</th>\n<th>Use the CDC and LFC seats to secure anticipatory-action and EOC funding.</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><p>A1.8 – Score 1</p>\n<p>(High)</p></th>\n<th>No State of Imminent Disaster procedure, RDRRMC request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template exists.</th>\n<th>Establish the RA 12287 anticipatory governance process (GOV-05).</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th><p>A2.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></th>\n<th>CDRRMO sections exist but not as an approved JMC 2014-1 structure; normal-operations and response tasks are mixed.</th>\n<th>Approve the permanent structure (Chapter 2).</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><p>B1.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></th>\n<th>No EO creates the EOC. Site visit (October 2026): a developing EOC, co-located with the OpCen in the CDRRM Office, runs 24-hour duty for City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring.</th>\n<th>Issue an EO establishing the EOC and its relationship with the CDRRMC, the OpCen and ICS.</th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th><p>B6.1 – Score 1</p>\n<p>(High)</p></th>\n<th>The ICS–EOC interface and chain of command are not defined.</th>\n<th>Separate incident command from EOC coordination.</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n*Note. Scores use the 0–4 IAT scale; priority is shown where it is High or Medium.*\n\nThe assessment also identifies broader system dependencies involving City Planning, Engineering, Budget, Environment, Social Welfare, Health, Education, Utilities and other institutions. These are addressed through the interface arrangements established in this chapter and the succeeding operational modules.\n\n**Management implication**: The priority is not simply to create more committees or issue additional policies. It is to establish a coherent governance system in which each mandate has an accountable owner, each process has a defined workflow, each decision has a legitimate authority, and each institutional output can be verified.\n\n**Assessment interpretation**: The workbook’s Dashboard (IAT v1.7, 11 Nov 2026) reports 1.98/4.00 as the combined Institutional Maturity Index, after the evidence rule (Methodology 5.2) was applied to 15 indicators. Part C is reported separately and is not included in that index. Unscored indicators and provisional desk assessments must not be read as proof of compliance.\n\n### 1.2 Maturity and Compliance in the Governance Domains\n\nThe assessment separates institutional maturity from mandatory compliance (Introduction, section 5). A domain can reach a fair maturity index and still be legally non-compliant because one or more Mandatory requirements are below 3. This matters for governance in Ormoc:\n\n- CDRRMO Legal Mandate and DRRM Governance (A1) has a maturity index of 2.47 out of 4.00 but only 66.7% mandatory compliance (4 of 6).\n\n- EOC Legal Basis and Concept of Operations (B1) has a maturity index of 2.23 but only 20% mandatory compliance (1 of 5).\n\n- Across Parts A and B, mandatory compliance is 32.9% against a combined maturity index of 1.98.\n\nImprovements must therefore address both capability and compliance. A practice carried out informally is not yet an institutional control; an executive order, organisational chart or facility does not establish compliance until the requirement is documented, approved, used and evidenced.\n\n### 1.3 What the Consultations and Records Show\n\nThe assessment methods are described in the Introduction (section 5.2). The findings below are those that bear on governance.\n\n**Stakeholder position.** All offices consulted (CDRRMO, CPDO, CSWDO and the City Administrator’s Office) share one aim: clear roles and responsibilities for each unit and accountable person, and that these are carried out. All agree that a Manual of Operations will help significantly. This chapter responds by giving each governance function an accountable owner, a workflow and a record.\n\n**Issuance review.** The review of 2021–2024 issuances in October 2026, extended to 1993–2025 in November 2026 (section 4.5), found the following points relevant to governance:\n\n- **Council composition.** The CDRRMC was reorganised by EO No. 2022-0006 and later by EO Nos. 2023-0041 and 2024-032A. The membership register shall carry only the latest EO and its amendments.\n\n- **Warning-driven actions are decided event by event.** Pre-emptive evacuation (CDRRMC Res. No. 09 s.2021) and suspension of work and classes (Res. No. 08 s.2021; Res. No. 06 s.2022) were each adopted by a separate resolution at the time of the event, although Ordinance No. 003, s. 2013 (forced evacuation) and Ordinance No. 135, s. 2023 (suspensions) already set the authority. A pre-set warning–action matrix (A6.2) citing these ordinances shall replace event-by-event resolutions.\n\n- **Declarations.** The SP adopted a State of Calamity for Typhoon Odette (SP Res. No. 2021-366). The CDRRMC recommended a one-year State of Calamity for the 2024 dengue outbreak (Res. No. 06 s.2024). See Process 4 (GOV-04).\n\n- **Odette response decisions.** The Council approved a PHP 2 M standby fund for prepositioning food and non-food items (Res. No. 11 s.2021) and asked LEYECO V to run its 2 MW standby generator during widespread outages (Res. No. 10 s.2021). It also sent cash aid from the 2021 Quick Response Fund to other LGUs (SP Res. Nos. 2021-365, -367 and -369). No standing mutual-aid agreement exists (A10.1).\n\n- **Planning and funding.** The Council approved the DRRM 10-Year Plan (Res. No. 01 s.2023), and EO No. 2022-00020 created the DRRM Plan Technical Working Group. The flood control and drainage master plan was funded in 2023 (Res. Nos. 03, 04 and 07 s.2023; about PHP 11.0 M), but no output was sighted.\n\n- **Records control.** The files of CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022 contain each other’s text; the CCCM Executive Order is filed as No. 263 but the scan reads 243; SP Res. No. 2021-357 still cites the pre-merger barangay count. These findings support GOV-02 and GOV-08.\n\n### 1.4 Staff Perspectives on DRRM Planning and Budgeting\n\nDuring the assessment, CDRRMO staff shared written reflections on how the office plans, budgets and coordinates, together with a proposal to strengthen the link with barangays (Chapter 2, section 10.2). The papers show a shared commitment to a risk-informed office. The assessment team read them alongside the City’s records, so that points the office already agrees on can be acted on now, and points that need more data can be settled together at the December 2026 validation workshop.\n\n**Table 1.2**\n\n*Themes Raised in Staff Reflections and What the Records Show*\n\n| **Theme**                                     | **What the Records Show**                                                                                                                                                                                       | **Agreed Way Forward**                                                        |\n|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Balance of the budget across thematic areas   | The 2022 Trust Fund plan gave 20% of the 70% component to prevention and mitigation, 55% to preparedness and 25% to response (Figure 1.2). No thematic split is on file yet for 2024–2026.                      | Table the 2024–2026 LDRRMF split by thematic area at the next CDRRMC meeting. |\n| Use of risk data in decisions                 | A CDRA exists (EO No. 2023-0025) but is not yet carried into zoning, project screening or investment-plan tagging. There is no loss database.                                                                   | Score every proposal against the CDRA and the LDRRMP (A3.2, C1.2).            |\n| Monitoring budget use against results         | No annual LDRRMP monitoring report or utilisation report is on file. Trust Fund balances of PHP 79.5 M (2024) and PHP 52.7 M (2025) suggest slow use.                                                           | Start a quarterly results-and-finance report (A10.4, A4.4).                   |\n| Preparedness items that need funding          | Hazard-specific contingency plans, the Public Service Continuity Plan, this Manual, partnerships and school programmes are all CDP 2024–2030 PPAs (2.4, 2.5, 2.10.3, 2.16). The gap is execution, not planning. | Track each PPA in the Action Plan.                                            |\n| Alignment with national and global frameworks | The DRRM 10-Year Plan follows the NDRRMC 2022 guidebook; the CDP adopts the UNDRR Ten Essentials. No LCCAP–LDRRMP crosswalk exists.                                                                             | Prepare the crosswalk with the CPDO.                                          |\n| Link between planning and budgeting           | No written budget cycle says who formulates and who executes the investment plan.                                                                                                                               | Adopt the budget-cycle RACI (Chapter 2, 16.2).                                |\n\n*Note. Records reviewed: CDRRMC resolutions 2021–2025, CDP 2024–2030, DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 and the IAT v1.7 workbook. No record reviewed raises concerns about how any section handles funds. The City’s Full Disclosure postings (A10.5) and its model-LGU partnerships (EO No. 2023-0026) are strengths to build on.*\n\n**Figure 1.2**\n\n*Allocation of the 70% Component of the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF by Thematic Area*\n\n![Figure 7: Allocation of the 70% Component of the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF by Thematic Area](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-07.webp)\n\n*Note. Data from the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF budget programme and investment plan (CDRRMC Res. No. 01 s.2022). The response share funded COVID-19 vaccines; preparedness was mostly vehicles; prevention and mitigation was data equipment. Comparable splits for 2024–2026 are not yet available.*\n\nThe following shared commitments are proposed for the office to confirm at the December 2026 workshop:\n\n- We will show how the LDRRMF splits across the four thematic areas, starting with 2024–2026, so that decisions rest on shared numbers.\n\n- Planning and execution need different strengths. Research and Planning brings risk evidence; Administration brings costing, procurement and audit discipline. The investment plan is strongest when both sign it.\n\n- Response readiness and long-term risk reduction both need funding. Scoring every proposal against the CDRA and the LDRRMP helps the office balance them.\n\n- Research and Planning will build one monitoring tool that every division reports into each quarter.\n\n- Research and Planning leads the formulation of the investment plan; Administration leads execution; the CDRRMO Head approves; the CDRRMC endorses.\n\n## 2. Purpose\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal and institutional governance arrangements governing the CDRRMO, CDRRMC and EOC.\n\nIt aims to:\n\n1.  Establish a clear and legally grounded division of authority among the Local Chief Executive, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n2.  Define the institutional responsibilities, decision rights and accountability of participating City Government offices.\n\n3.  Institutionalise transparent, inclusive and evidence-based DRRM governance.\n\n4.  Establish procedures for CDRRMC meetings, resolutions, recommendations and action monitoring.\n\n5.  Standardise the City’s procedures for State of Calamity and State of Imminent Disaster declarations.\n\n6.  Strengthen vertical and horizontal coordination among the City Government, barangays, national government agencies, neighbouring LGUs, CSOs, private-sector partners and humanitarian organisations.\n\n7.  Establish process ownership, responsibility assignments and decision-making controls using the RACI framework.\n\n8.  Embed risk-informed governance, quality assurance, performance monitoring and continuous improvement into institutional operations.\n\n## 3. Scope and Application\n\nThis chapter applies to the following institutional actors:\n\n- Office of the City Mayor;\n\n- CDRRMC and its Secretariat;\n\n- CDRRMO Head and functional divisions;\n\n- EOC Manager and EOC functional sections;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod;\n\n- City Government departments, offices and units;\n\n- Barangay DRRMCs in all 85 barangays;\n\n- National government agencies and regional DRRM institutions;\n\n- Accredited CSOs, ACDVs, private-sector organisations and other partners.\n\nIts provisions apply under three operating conditions:\n\n**Table 1.3**\n\n*Scope and Application*\n\n| **Operating Condition**                       | **Institutional Focus**                                                                                                                                | **Primary Governance Mechanism**                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Normal operations                             | Risk reduction, institutional planning, preparedness, capacity development, monitoring and continuity.                                                 | CDRRMC oversight and CDRRMO management.                                           |\n| Heightened preparedness / anticipatory action | Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment, warning-action decisions, resource readiness, anticipatory measures and possible State of Imminent Disaster declaration. | CDRRMC coordination, Mayor’s executive authority and CDRRMO technical leadership. |\n| Emergency operations                          | Incident coordination, situational awareness, resource mobilisation, policy support and operational reporting.                                         | Activated EOC, Incident Management Team and designated response clusters.         |\n\nThe Manual applies to all hazards and emergencies within the City’s jurisdiction, including hydrometeorological, geological, climate-related, technological and human-induced hazards, subject to applicable institutional mandates.\n\nIt shall be read together with the City’s approved DRRM plans, contingency plans, Public Service Continuity Plan, climate and disaster risk assessment, development plans, land-use plans and other applicable local policies.\n\n## 4. Legal, Policy and Standards Framework\n\nThe institutional arrangements of this chapter are interpreted and implemented within the hierarchy of instruments below. The Introduction to the Manual (section 3) sets out the legal and policy basis and its principal national anchors; this section lists the core instruments and gives the full framework, including the City’s own issuances.\n\n### 4.1 Philippine Legal and Regulatory Framework\n\n**Table 1.4**\n\n*Philippine Laws and Their Application to DRRM Governance*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                 | **Relevant Provisions**                                         | **Application to This Chapter**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Republic Act No. 10121 (2010)                                  | Sections 11–17 and 21                                           | CDRRMC and CDRRMO, emergency coordination, State of Calamity and LDRRMF governance.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| IRR of RA 10121                                                | Rules on local councils, offices, coordination and declarations | Operational detail of the statutory responsibilities.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| RA 7160 (1991), Local Government Code                          | Sections 16, 33, 76, 444 and 458                                | General welfare, inter-LGU cooperation, organisational structure, executive and legislative authority.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| RA 12287 (2025), Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act | Sections 3–7 and 18                                             | Signed into law on 12 September 2025. Pre-disaster risk assessment, declaration by EO on the prescribed recommendation, anticipatory action, automatic lifting when the hazard occurs.                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| IRR of RA 12287 (2026)                                         | Rules I–VII                                                     | Signed on 14 September 2026; effective upon publication and filing with the UP Office of the National Administrative Register. Lead time of three (3) days, extendable to five (5); AA fund up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component; tranche rule for hydrometeorological and geological hazards; 45-day report. Rule IV has ten sections and states no count of actions. |\n| RA 9729 (2009), as amended by RA 10174                         | Climate change adaptation and financing                         | Climate resilience in DRRM governance and planning.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| RA 10173 (2012), Data Privacy Act                              | Processing and protection of personal information               | Affected-population, beneficiary, responder and institutional information.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| RA 9710 (2009), Magna Carta of Women                           | Gender-responsive governance                                    | Gender-responsive and inclusive arrangements.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| RA 7277, as amended                                            | Rights and accessibility of persons with disabilities           | Accessible governance, warning and emergency services.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| RA 9994 (2010)                                                 | Senior citizens                                                 | Inclusion of older persons in DRRM decisions and services.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| RA 10821 (2016)                                                | Children’s Emergency Relief and Protection Act                  | Child protection; limits on schools as evacuation centres.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| RA 11036 (2018), Mental Health Act                             | Mental health services                                          | Institutional and responder psychosocial support.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| RA 12076 (2024), Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act                      | Permanent evacuation centres                                    | Evacuation infrastructure standards.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| RA 12009 (2024), New Government Procurement Act                | Procurement, including emergency procurement                    | Resource governance and procurement accountability (Chapter 6).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| RA 9418 (2007), Volunteer Act                                  | Local volunteer programmes (Sec. 12)                            | Basis of Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024 (section 4.5).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n### 4.2 Administrative and Operational Guidelines\n\n**Table 1.5**\n\n*National Administrative and Operational Issuances*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                             | **Application**                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| NDRRMC–DILG–DBM–CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                                         | LDRRMO structure, staffing and functions: Head and three functional areas (Administration and Training, Research and Planning, Operations and Warning).                                                                   |\n| NDRRMC–DBM–DILG JMC No. 2013-1; DBM Local Budget Circular No. 124                          | LDRRMF programming and use.                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002                                                                  | Accounting and reporting of the LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund. The section governing trust-fund transfers is cited differently in secondary sources (4.4 or 5.1.10); verify against the circular before quoting it.       |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012                                                  | Institutionalisation and use of the Incident Command System.                                                                                                                                                              |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 (25 October 2018)                                       | EOC establishment, activation triggers, five management functions, non-co-location of the EOC and the incident command post, and LGU EOC funding chargeable to the LDRRMF. It allows each EOC to set its own alert codes. |\n| National Disaster Response Plan, as restated in RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023 | White, Blue and Red alert levels: White, normal operations; Blue, at least 50% of resources on standby; Red, all resources available.                                                                                     |\n| DILG VIII Memorandum R08-LGCDD-2025-02-26-008 (26 February 2025)                           | Harmonised pre-disaster, during, post-disaster and terminal reporting; LGU-to-Province cut-offs.                                                                                                                          |\n| NDCC Memorandum Circular No. 12, s. 2008                                                   | Cluster-based disaster response coordination.                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| OCD/NDRRMC Public Service Continuity Planning Guidebook                                    | Continuity of essential functions, business impact analysis, alternate arrangements and succession.                                                                                                                       |\n| NDRRMC MC No. 3, s. 2025; OCD Memorandum No. 763, s. 2026 (corrected)                      | Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 criteria and tiers (Gold 2.50 and above; Silver 2.00–2.49; Bronze 1.50–1.99).                                                                                                                       |\n| NPC Circular 16-03; NPC Circular 2023-06                                                   | Personal data breach management (72-hour notification); security of personal data.                                                                                                                                        |\n| DILG Operation L!sto and applicable annual issuances                                       | Preparedness and institutional readiness of LGUs and barangays.                                                                                                                                                           |\n\nJMC No. 2014-1 recognises the LDRRMO as a mandatory office with a Head and three core functional areas and permits augmentation of staffing, subject to the applicable requirements. City Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014, as amended, adopts this structure (section 4.5).\n\n### 4.3 International Frameworks and Management Standards\n\n**Table 1.6**\n\n*International Frameworks and Management Standards*\n\n| **Framework / Standard**                                                          | **Application to Institutional Governance**                                                       |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030                            | Risk governance, understanding risk, investing in resilience, preparedness and Build Back Better. |\n| UNDRR Making Cities Resilient 2030 – Ten Essentials                               | Urban resilience governance and institutional performance.                                        |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities – Detailed Level Assessment (2017) | Diagnostic reference for institutional and city-wide resilience (Part C indicators).              |\n| ISO 31000:2018 – Risk management                                                  | Risk identification, analysis, treatment, monitoring and communication in governance.             |\n| ISO 9001:2015 – Quality management systems                                        | Process ownership, documented procedures, performance evaluation and continual improvement.       |\n| ISO 22301:2019 – Business continuity management systems                           | Continuity of essential services.                                                                 |\n| Sphere Handbook (2018)                                                            | Humanitarian quality and accountability.                                                          |\n| CReST and systems-thinking approaches                                             | Interdependencies, feedback loops, cascading impacts and system-level interventions.              |\n\nThese frameworks complement Philippine law; they do not replace it. Where a standard is more demanding than the statutory minimum, the City may adopt it as a good-practice benchmark within its authority and resources.\n\n### 4.4 Policy Hierarchy and Conflict Resolution\n\nIn implementing this chapter, the following order applies:\n\n1.  Philippine Constitution and applicable statutes;\n\n2.  Valid implementing rules and regulations;\n\n3.  National administrative and operational issuances;\n\n4.  Provincial and regional coordination requirements, where applicable;\n\n5.  City ordinances and valid executive issuances;\n\n6.  Approved plans, policies and institutional procedures;\n\n7.  MOO protocols, checklists and supporting forms.\n\nWhere instruments conflict, the higher-ranking one prevails. The CDRRMO refers material legal ambiguities to the City Legal Office and seeks technical clarification from OCD Region VIII or the competent national agency. The legal and policy register (section 4.5 and Annex 1-I) names a custodian and a review date for each instrument.\n\n### 4.5 Local DRRM Issuances of the City of Ormoc\n\nWhen the first drafts of this chapter were written, City staff were still compiling the local issuances. They are now held in the City’s shared folder of local policies, in four sets: SP ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC resolutions and SP resolutions, each filed by year. Each document has also been registered on its own in the MODE Vault. Table 1.7 summarises what has been obtained; Annex 1-I lists every instrument.\n\n**Table 1.7**\n\n*Local DRRM Issuances Obtained, by Type (as of 12 November 2026)*\n\n| **Type**                                           | **Number Registered** | **Years Covered**                                          | **Issuing Body**         |\n|----------------------------------------------------|-----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| SP ordinances (including appropriation ordinances) | 28                    | 1993, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2017, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025 | Sangguniang Panlungsod   |\n| Executive orders                                   | 14                    | 2016, 2017, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024                   | Office of the City Mayor |\n| CDRRMC resolutions                                 | 67                    | 2016, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025             | City DRRM Council        |\n| SP resolutions                                     | 12                    | 2021, 2023, 2024                                           | Sangguniang Panlungsod   |\n| Total                                              | 121                   | 1993–2025                                                  |                          |\n\n*Note. Counted from the MODE Vault register (supabase/seed/operational/04_vault_register.sql), which registers each file in the City’s Drive folders; seven ordinance scans were read in full on 12 November 2026 to confirm titles and dates. Duplicate filings of the same instrument are counted once.*\n\n#### 4.5.1 Issuances That Establish or Govern the DRRM System\n\n**Table 1.8 lists the local instruments that this Manual relies on. The others in Annex 1-I record individual decisions (budgets, realignments, assistance to other LGUs) and are evidence of practice rather than standing rules.**\n\n**Table 1.8**\n\n*Local Issuances That Establish or Govern the City DRRM System*\n\n| **Instrument and Date**                                                                                                       | **What It Provides**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Use in the Manual**                                                                                                                                                        |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Ordinance No. 37, s. 1993 (18 Nov 1993)                                                                                       | Declares four high-risk areas after the 1991 flash flood: the Alegria–Punta shoreline (1.48 ha), Anilao river banks Nos. 1 and 2 (16.07 ha and 0.2 ha) and Malbasag river banks (6.50 ha). Habitation and construction are prohibited; no building permit may issue; existing structures were to be removed within three years after notice.                                                                                                                                             | Risk-informed land use (Chapter 3); Build Back Better screening (Chapter 5). Boundaries are cadastral; check them against the CDRA, CLUP and Zoning Ordinance with the CPDO. |\n| Ordinance No. 003, s. 2013 (3 Oct 2013)                                                                                       | Pre-emptive or Forced Evacuation Ordinance. On CDRRMC recommendation, the Mayor declares forced evacuation, designates evacuation centres and staging areas, informs residents in the local dialect and enlists PNP, AFP, BFP and BJMP; reasonable force may be used with attention to vulnerable groups; the CDRRMO trains residents yearly.                                                                                                                                            | Evacuation authority in Chapter 4 (SOP 4-06); A1.5 notes it needs triggers and procedural steps.                                                                             |\n| Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (26 Jun 2014), amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015 (12 Mar 2015)                                 | Establishes the CDRRMO as a mandatory office under the Office of the City Mayor and a BDRRMC in each barangay; repeals Ordinance No. 2012-176. The Head is a City Government Assistant Department Head I (SG-23) with three Local DRRM Officer I (SG-11) posts for Administration and Training, Research and Planning, and Operations and Warning; staff may be augmented by designation, reassignment, detail or transfer of vacant items. Lists the office’s functions under RA 10121. | Legal basis of the CDRRMO (A1.1, A2.1–A2.3). The office exists by ordinance; what remains is department status and a fuller plantilla (section 4.5.2).                       |\n| EO No. 26, s. 2017 and EO No. 27, s. 2017                                                                                     | Create the City Incident Command System and the CDRRMO Operations Center.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | ICS and OpCen basis (B1.1, B4.1); confirm whether still in force against the proposed EOC establishment EO (Annex 1-C).                                                      |\n| EO No. 2024-032A (July 2024), preceded by EO No. 28, s. 2016; No. 14, s. 2019; No. 151, s. 2021; No. 2022-0006; No. 2023-0041 | Current CDRRMC composition.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Membership register carries only the latest EO and its amendments (A1.2).                                                                                                    |\n| EO No. 2022-00020                                                                                                             | Creates the TWG for formulation, implementation, monitoring and evaluation of the City DRRM Plan, with thematic focal offices.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | LDRRMP and M&E (Chapters 3 and 7).                                                                                                                                           |\n| EO s. 2022 (filed as No. 263; the scan reads 243)                                                                             | Organises the City CCCM Team under the CDRRMC Response Cluster, led by the CSWDO.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Camp management (Chapter 4); confirm the number.                                                                                                                             |\n| EO Nos. 2023-0025, 2023-0026, 2023-0042, 2023-0085                                                                            | CDRA TWG; Project TRANSFORM TWG; El Niño Task Force; Barangay DRRM Plan Review Team.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Risk assessment, slow-onset hazards and BDRRMP quality (Chapter 3).                                                                                                          |\n| Ordinance No. 135, s. 2023, amended by Ordinance No. 189, s. 2025 (8 Apr 2025)                                                | Guidelines on cancelling or suspending government work and classes in disasters and emergencies.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Warning-to-action decisions (Chapter 4, SOP 4-02).                                                                                                                           |\n| Ordinance No. 102, s. 2023 (28 Feb 2023)                                                                                      | Institutionalises the City Epidemiology and Surveillance Unit under the City Health Department.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Health hazards and epidemics (Chapters 3 and 4).                                                                                                                             |\n| Ordinance No. 160, s. 2024 (13 Aug 2024)                                                                                      | Institutionalises the City volunteer programme under RA 9418.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | Volunteers and ACDVs (Chapters 2 and 9).                                                                                                                                     |\n| Ordinance No. 171, s. 2024 (26 Nov 2024)                                                                                      | Institutionalises Barangay Health Emergency Response Teams in all barangays.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Barangay health response (Chapters 4 and 9).                                                                                                                                 |\n| CDRRMC Res. No. 09, s. 2024                                                                                                   | Adopts ICS as the standard management system for planned and unplanned events.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | ICS training and use (Chapter 4).                                                                                                                                            |\n| CDRRMC Res. No. 01, s. 2023; SP Res. No. 2023-112                                                                             | Approval and adoption of the DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | LDRRMP (Chapter 3).                                                                                                                                                          |\n| CDRRMC Res. No. 05, s. 2025                                                                                                   | 5% LDRRMF CY 2026 budget programme and investment plan (PHP 139,955,741.40).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | LDRRMF (Chapter 6).                                                                                                                                                          |\n| SP Res. No. 2024-102; SP Res. No. 2023-347; SP Res. No. 2021-036                                                              | Omnibus authority to accept donations; MOA with a volunteer rescue group; MOA with the National Resilience Council.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Donations and partnerships (Chapters 6 and 8).                                                                                                                               |\n| CDRRMC Res. Nos. 2-A and 2-B, s. 2021                                                                                         | Adopt the enhanced flood (“Oplan Lunop”) and earthquake (2020–2025) contingency plans.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | Contingency plans (Chapter 3); the earthquake plan period has ended.                                                                                                         |\n\n*Note. Summaries of instruments marked with a date were confirmed from the scans on 12 November 2026; the others follow the Vault register. The official text governs.*\n\n#### 4.5.2 What the Issuances Show\n\n- **The CDRRMO already exists by ordinance.** Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014, as amended, created the office in 2014 under the Office of the City Mayor, with the JMC 2014-1 functions. It was not among the documents reviewed in October 2026, so the A1.1 finding that “an Executive Order is the only basis” must be revisited at validation. The remaining gap is narrower: the office is not a regular department and its plantilla is the JMC 2014-1 minimum. The proposed institutionalisation ordinance (Annex 1-A) should therefore be recast as an amendment of Ordinance No. 008.\n\n- **Evacuation authority is written down.** Ordinance No. 003, s. 2013 gives the Mayor the power to declare forced evacuation on CDRRMC recommendation. Pre-emptive evacuation for Typhoon Odette (CDRRMC Res. No. 09, s. 2021) still went through an event-specific resolution; the warning-to-action matrix in Chapter 4 should cite the ordinance instead.\n\n- **A no-build rule for high-risk areas has been in force since 1993.** Ordinance No. 37, s. 1993 predates the CDRA and the CLUP 2017–2027 and does not appear in either. Its boundaries need to be mapped and reconciled with current hazard maps.\n\n- **The Council has been reorganised six times in ten years** (2016, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2023 and 2024). Only the latest EO is the membership basis.\n\n- **Most CDRRMC resolutions are financial or event-specific.** Of the 67 CDRRMC resolutions on file, most approve budgets, realignments, supplemental appropriations or assistance. Standing policy is set mainly by ordinances and EOs.\n\n- **Health emergency capacity is institutionalised at city and barangay level** through Ordinance Nos. 102, s. 2023 and 171, s. 2024, which the Manual should link to the response clusters.\n\n#### 4.5.3 Gaps and Records Control\n\n**Table 1.9**\n\n*Issuance Years Not Obtained*\n\n| **Set and Year**              | **Status**                                                           |\n|-------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| SP Ordinance No. 135, s. 2023 | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| CDRRMC Resolutions, s. 2017   | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| CDRRMC Resolutions, s. 2018   | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| Executive Orders, s. 2018     | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| Executive Orders, s. 2020     | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| Executive Orders, s. 2021     | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| Executive Orders, s. 2025     | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| Executive Orders, s. 2026     | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n| SP Resolutions, 2025          | Not obtained; the year folder on the City Drive is empty or missing. |\n\n*Note. Gaps are audit evidence. Either obtain the missing years from the SP Secretariat and the Office of the City Mayor, or record “not obtained” in the Vault register (SOP GOV-02 and GOV-08).*\n\nRecords-control issues already found in the issuance set: the files of CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04, s. 2022 contain each other’s text; the CCCM EO is filed as No. 263 but the scan reads 243; one SP resolution file name (2024-1027) differs from the document number (2024-102); CDRRMC Res. No. 04, s. 2025 is undated and repeats a control number; and EO No. 151, s. 2021 has a blank date line. The CDRRMO Secretariat corrects the register, not the original documents.\n\n## 5. Systems-Thinking Framework for DRRM Governance\n\n### 5.1 Institutional System Architecture\n\nOrmoc City’s DRRM governance shall operate as a connected system comprising five interdependent layers:\n\n1.  **Executive governance**: Political leadership, statutory authority and executive decision-making.\n\n2.  **Policy and oversigh**t: CDRRMC deliberation, policy direction, recommendations and monitoring.\n\n3.  **Institutional management**: CDRRMO planning, technical management, administration and implementation.\n\n4.  **Operational coordination**: EOC, ICS, response clusters and incident management arrangements.\n\n5.  **Community and external interface:** BDRRMCs, CSOs, private-sector institutions, utilities, national agencies and neighbouring LGUs.\n\n**Figure 1.3**\n\n*The Five-Layer Institutional Architecture of DRRM Governance*\n\n![Figure 8: The Five-Layer Institutional Architecture of DRRM Governance](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-08.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n**Conceptual relationship**. This is not a substitute for the statutory chain of authority or the approved ICS organisational chart.\n\nThe architecture recognises that information, authority and resources move through different pathways.\n\n- Executive authority flows through legally established government structures.\n\n- Technical information flows from monitoring institutions, technical offices and communities toward decision-makers.\n\n- Operational instructions flow through the approved command and coordination arrangements.\n\n- Resources flow through authorised mobilisation and accountability mechanisms.\n\n- Feedback and performance information flow back into planning, policy and institutional improvement.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall ensure that these pathways are connected but not conflated.\n\n### 5.2 The CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Interface\n\nThe assessment identifies the institutional interface as the weakest component, with a separate maturity index of 1.41/4.00.\n\nThis indicates that the problem is not limited to individual office capacity. It concerns the connections between institutional actors, their processes, information systems and decision-making arrangements.\n\nThe following operating distinctions shall therefore apply:\n\n**Table 1.10**\n\n*The CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Interface*\n\n| **Dimension**            | **CDRRMO**                                                                                     | **EOC**                                                                                   |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Institutional status     | Permanent LGU office                                                                           | Operational coordination mechanism established through appropriate local issuance         |\n| Operating condition      | Continuous normal operations                                                                   | Activated according to approved triggers and authority                                    |\n| Primary accountability   | CDRRMO Head                                                                                    | Designated EOC Manager                                                                    |\n| Main function            | DRRM governance, planning, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery support | Incident coordination, information management, resource coordination and decision support |\n| Planning horizon         | Strategic, annual and multi-year                                                               | Incident-specific and operational-period based                                            |\n| Information              | Maintains institutional risk and readiness information                                         | Consolidates and validates current incident information                                   |\n| Relationship with ICS    | Provides institutional support and coordinates preparedness arrangements                       | Supports the Incident Commander and Incident Management Team                              |\n| Institutional continuity | Maintains permanent office functions                                                           | Operates under activation, deactivation and continuity arrangements                       |\n\nThe EOC shall not replace the CDRRMO. Nor shall it become an alternative command structure for field response.\n\nThe Incident Commander shall retain incident-level command authority under the applicable ICS arrangements. The EOC shall facilitate coordination, provide information, mobilise support and elevate decisions requiring executive action.\n\n### 5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies\n\nPart C of the assessment identifies 15 institutional interface indicators. These are deliberately excluded from the CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Maturity Index because the primary operational ownership rests with other offices or external institutions.\n\n**Figure 1.4**\n\n*System Dependencies: CDRRMO Coordinates, Sector Offices Keep Their Mandates*\n\n![Figure 9: System Dependencies: CDRRMO Coordinates, Sector Offices Keep Their Mandates](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-09.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\nThe distinction is important for accountability.\n\n**Table 1.11**\n\n*Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies*\n\n| **Interface Area**                                                  | **Primary Institutional Owner**                       | **CDRRMO’s Role**                                                                |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk-informed CDP/CLUP priorities and scenarios                     | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO)           | Supply risk evidence and provide DRR/CCA technical advice.                       |\n| DRR/CCA screening of projects and budgets                           | CPDO and City Budget Office                           | Provide hazard and resilience screening inputs.                                  |\n| Damage, livelihood and ecosystem loss analysis                      | CPDO and relevant sector offices                      | Supply disaster risk and impact information.                                     |\n| Hazard-sensitive zoning and building permits                        | CPDO and Zoning Administrator                         | Provide technical hazard information and participate in review.                  |\n| EOC, evacuation center and critical-facility structural assessments | City Engineer / Building Official                     | Identify critical facilities and coordinate risk prioritisation.                 |\n| Power, water and telecommunications continuity                      | Utility providers and relevant City offices           | Coordinate restoration priorities and continuity agreements.                     |\n| Transport anticipatory action                                       | City Engineer, traffic office, DPWH and port operator | Integrate transport risk information into emergency coordination.                |\n| Watershed and ecosystem protection                                  | CENRO and DENR                                        | Integrate ecosystem-related hazards and protective functions into DRRM planning. |\n| Health anticipatory action                                          | City Health Office and hospitals                      | Coordinate the Health Cluster and operational information.                       |\n| Education continuity                                                | DepEd Schools Division                                | Coordinate school preparedness and learning-continuity arrangements.             |\n| Agriculture and fisheries anticipatory action                       | City Agriculture Office and City Veterinary Office    | Coordinate hazard information and response integration.                          |\n| Pre-impact price monitoring                                         | DTI and City Market Office                            | Facilitate information sharing with the EOC.                                     |\n| Business continuity outreach                                        | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce                        | Facilitate risk communication and preparedness outreach.                         |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall not assume technical or statutory responsibilities assigned to these offices. Its role is to establish the coordination mechanisms, information exchange and performance monitoring necessary for integrated city-wide resilience.\n\nThis distinction is essential to avoid institutional duplication and unfunded mandates.\n\n## 6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates\n\n**Figure 1.5**\n\n*Statutory Mandates and Decision Rights of the Mayor, Sanggunian, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC*\n\n![Figure 10: Statutory Mandates and Decision Rights of the Mayor, Sanggunian, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-10.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n### 6.1 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council\n\nThe CDRRMC shall serve as the City’s principal DRRM policy-making, coordinating and oversight body under RA 10121.\n\nIts composition and functions shall conform to Section 11 of the Act and its IRR.\n\nThe City shall maintain an updated Executive Order identifying the membership, institutional representation, designated alternates and coordination responsibilities of the Council.\n\nThe assessment (IAT v1.7, A1.2) identifies the following governance requirements:\n\n- Issuing an amending EO to EO 2024-032A, the current reorganisation order;\n\n- Keeping the CSO seats (five under EO 2024-032A) supported by SP accreditation of each CSO;\n\n- Filling the vacant third private-sector seat and restoring an academe seat (the City College of Ormoc, recommended by CDRRMC Res. No. 10 s.2024);\n\n- Maintaining institutional representation rather than relying solely on individual appointments; and\n\n- Reviewing the membership register annually.\n\nThe CDRRMC shall ensure representation from relevant City Government offices, national agencies, public safety institutions, CSOs, the private sector and other prescribed members.\n\nIts principal functions shall include:\n\n1.  Approving, monitoring and evaluating the Local DRRM Plan;\n\n2.  Integrating DRR and climate change adaptation into local development plans and budgets;\n\n3.  Providing strategic direction for DRRM programmes and policies;\n\n4.  Recommending pre-emptive and forced evacuation where warranted;\n\n5.  Recommending a State of Calamity declaration to the appropriate local legislative authority;\n\n6.  Coordinating anticipatory action under RA 12287;\n\n7.  Mobilising institutional and external resources;\n\n8.  Monitoring the use of DRRM funds and other dedicated resources; and\n\n9.  Reviewing institutional performance, assessment findings and corrective actions.\n\n### 6.2 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office\n\nThe CDRRMO shall function as the City’s permanent institutional and technical mechanism for DRRM.\n\nUnder Section 12 of RA 10121, it shall be responsible for setting the direction, development, implementation and coordination of local DRRM programmes.\n\nIts responsibilities include:\n\n- Risk assessment and local risk information management;\n\n- Local DRRM planning and contingency planning;\n\n- Multi-hazard early warning;\n\n- Training, orientation and knowledge management;\n\n- Resource and capability inventory;\n\n- Coordination with communities, CSOs and private-sector partners;\n\n- Emergency monitoring and mobilisation;\n\n- Organisation and supervision of emergency response teams and ACDVs;\n\n- Recovery coordination;\n\n- CDRRMC Secretariat functions; and\n\n- Preparation and reporting of DRRM plans, budgets and fund utilisation.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall perform these responsibilities through its approved organisational structure and in coordination with other City Government offices.\n\n### 6.3 Emergency Operations Center\n\nThe EOC shall provide the City’s centralised mechanism for operational coordination during emergencies.\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\n1.  Maintain situational awareness;\n\n2.  Consolidate, validate and disseminate incident information;\n\n3.  Support CDRRMC and executive decision-making;\n\n4.  Facilitate coordination among the Incident Management Team and response clusters;\n\n5.  Coordinate resource requests and mobilisation;\n\n6.  Maintain incident and decision records;\n\n7.  Facilitate vertical coordination with provincial and regional DRRM institutions, as applicable; and\n\n8.  Support transition from response to recovery.\n\nThe EOC shall operate within the authority established by the Mayor and the approved institutional arrangements.\n\n**Scenario-based activation.** The EOC is one fixed structure, separate from the CDRRMO office chart (Figure 1.6). Each contingency plan switches on only the modules its scenario needs (Figure 1.7). CDRRMO divisions supply the section chiefs, partner offices staff the cluster desks, and the proposed cluster coordinators form the Barangay Liaison Desk. The Incident Commander commands on site; the EOC coordinates and supplies; the EOC Manager elevates policy decisions to the CDRRMC and the Mayor.\n\n**Figure 1.6**\n\n*Proposed City EOC Structure and Its Relationship With Incident Command*\n\n![Figure 11: Proposed City EOC Structure and Its Relationship With Incident Command](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-11.webp)\n\n*Note. Proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop, following NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018. AA = anticipatory action; IMT = incident management team; MDM = management of the dead and missing; PDRA = pre-disaster risk assessment; QRF = Quick Response Fund; WASH = water, sanitation and hygiene.*\n\n**Table 1.12**\n\n*EOC Activation Levels, Staffing and Provincial Reporting Times*\n\n| **Level**      | **Who Staffs the EOC**                                                                                                                                        | **Reports to the Province (DILG VIII Cut-Offs)** |\n|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| White (normal) | OpCen duty team, 2 per shift; EOC Manager on call.                                                                                                            | 6 AM and 6 PM                                    |\n| Blue (alert)   | Adds the EOC Manager, Planning (situation unit, PDRA and AA desk) and the Logistics lead. Cluster focal persons and the Barangay Liaison Desk are on standby. | 9 AM and 7 PM                                    |\n| Red (full)     | All EOC sections, command staff, cluster desks and six barangay desks; 12-hour shifts, two deep.                                                              | 9 AM and 7 PM, and as events require             |\n\n*Note. At Red the EOC needs about 50 people over two shifts, more than the CDRRMO can field alone. Chapter 2, section 10.1 sets out how pre-designated augmentees from City offices close the gap.*\n\n**Figure 1.7**\n\n*EOC Modules Activated by Contingency Scenario*\n\n![Figure 12: EOC Modules Activated by Contingency Scenario](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-12.webp)\n\n*Note. Proposed; each contingency plan sets its exact triggers. “Lead clusters” means only the clusters that lead that scenario are seated. Typhoon: Blue on forecast landfall, Red at Wind Signal No. 2 or a flood or storm-surge warning; the RA 12287 path applies when the lead time is at least three (3) days (IRR; extendable to five days). Flash flood: Red on an orange or red rainfall warning; usually below the 72-hour floor, so city early action applies. Earthquake: Red automatically on strong shaking; move to the alternate EOC if the primary is damaged. El Niño: White or Blue only, coordinated by the El Niño Task Force (EO No. 2023-0042). Disease outbreak: Blue, Red at the Health Cluster’s request. Industrial incident, fire or planned event: Blue as needed, with the IMT under CDRRMC Res. No. 09 s.2024.*\n\n**Geospatial common operating picture.** The EOC shall keep a geospatial common operating picture (Geo-COP) as part of its information-management function. Its reference point is the EOC location registered from the KML supplied for this Manual (11.013896° N, 124.604287° E), which the site check under IAT B2.1 used for the hazard-map overlay. The facility entrance and footprint still need a field survey. Partner offices keep ownership of their own map layers; the CDRRMO maintains the system. The Geo-COP supports coordination and does not transfer command from the Incident Commander. The full procedure (SOP 6-GIS-01) and its forms belong to the EOC information-management provisions of Chapter 4 (section 11), with the EOC facility in Chapter 2.\n\nThese arrangements address IAT items B1.4 (concept of operations), B4.1 (EOC structure), B4.4 (liaison desks) and B6.1 (ICS–EOC interface) on paper. The scores rise once the EOC Executive Order is issued and an exercise tests the structure (B7.3).\n\n### 6.4 Office of the City Mayor\n\nThe City Mayor shall exercise the powers and responsibilities vested in the Local Chief Executive under RA 7160, RA 10121, RA 12287 and other applicable laws.\n\nThe Mayor shall:\n\n- Provide executive leadership and direction;\n\n- Chair the CDRRMC;\n\n- Authorise actions within statutory authority;\n\n- Direct the mobilisation of City Government offices and resources;\n\n- Issue valid executive orders and administrative directives;\n\n- Exercise the relevant declaration authority under RA 12287;\n\n- Approve institutional arrangements within the scope of executive authority; and\n\n- Elevate matters requiring legislative or higher-level government action.\n\nThe Mayor’s executive authority shall remain distinct from the legislative authority of the Sangguniang Panlungsod.\n\n### 6.5 Sangguniang Panlungsod\n\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall exercise its legislative and oversight responsibilities under RA 7160 and applicable DRRM legislation.\n\nIts relevant functions include:\n\n- Enacting the ordinance establishing or strengthening the CDRRMO;\n\n- Approving the necessary organisational and budgetary measures;\n\n- Considering CDRRMC recommendations for a State of Calamity declaration;\n\n- Enacting relevant local DRRM ordinances;\n\n- Exercising oversight over local DRRM policies and resource allocation; and\n\n- Supporting the institutionalisation of the City’s resilience priorities.\n\nLegal distinction: The declaration of a State of Calamity under Section 16 of RA 10121 is issued by the local sanggunian upon the recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on damage assessment and needs analysis. It is not simply a Mayor’s Executive Order.\n\n## 7. Institutional Governance Processes and Workflows\n\nThe MOO shall define governance as a series of connected, controlled and measurable processes. Each process shall have:\n\n- A defined trigger or initiating condition;\n\n- A designated process owner;\n\n- Responsible actors;\n\n- A legally authorised decision-maker;\n\n- Required inputs and evidence;\n\n- Defined outputs and records;\n\n- Escalation and exception procedures; and\n\n- Performance and compliance indicators.\n\nThis approach ensures that governance decisions can be traced from initiation to approval, implementation, reporting and review.\n\n### 7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-Making\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that DRRM policies, plans, priorities and institutional decisions are deliberated, approved or recommended by the appropriate authority and translated into accountable actions.\n\n**Figure 1.8**\n\n*Council Deliberation and Secretariat Management as One Policy Process*\n\n![Figure 13: Council Deliberation and Secretariat Management as One Policy Process](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-13.webp)\n\n*Note. Source: MODE platform image library (public/library/ch1v12), prepared for this chapter. Conceptual illustration; the chapter text governs.*\n\n**Workflow GOV-01: CDRRMC Governance**\n\n1.  **Initiation and agenda development**\n    > \\> CDRRMO identifies policy issues, assessment findings, risk developments and matters requiring Council action.\n\n2.  **Technical preparation**\n    > \\> Concerned offices submit technical papers, evidence, financial implications and recommendations.\n\n3.  \\*\\*Secretariat processing\n    > \\>\\*\\* CDRRMO consolidates agenda items, issues meeting notices and circulates supporting documents.\n\n4.  \\*\\*Council deliberation\n    > \\>\\*\\* CDRRMC members review evidence, assess implications and deliberate on proposed actions.\n\n5.  **Decision and documentation**\n    > \\> The Council approves matters within its authority or adopts recommendations for submission to the appropriate approving authority.\n\n6.  \\*\\*Implementation and assignment\n    > \\>\\*\\* The Secretariat records decisions, assigns action owners and establishes target dates.\n\n7.  \\*\\*Monitoring and feedback\n    > \\>\\*\\* CDRRMO tracks implementation and reports unresolved actions at succeeding meetings.\n\n**Required Output**\n\nApproved minutes, resolutions or recommendations, decision register and action-monitoring report.\n\n**Operating Requirements**\n\n**Table 1.13**\n\n*Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-Making*\n\n| **Control Point**    | **Required Standard**                                                                                                     |\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Meeting frequency    | At least once every three months and as necessary.                                                                        |\n| Agenda               | Include standing items on risk developments, LDRRMP implementation, funding, assessment gaps and institutional readiness. |\n| Supporting documents | Technical papers, risk information, financial implications and draft resolutions, as applicable.                          |\n| Documentation        | Minutes, attendance, decisions, dissenting views where material, assigned owners and deadlines.                           |\n| Follow-through       | Action register reviewed at every succeeding meeting.                                                                     |\n| Review               | Annual assessment of Council functionality and effectiveness.                                                             |\n\nThe assessment records a score of 4 for A1.3. This established practice should be retained and strengthened through systematic action tracking, particularly for the 30 high-priority institutional gaps.\n\n### 7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that Council proceedings, decisions and institutional records are complete, traceable and accessible to authorised stakeholders.\n\n**Workflow GOV-02: Secretariat Operations**\n\n1.  Receive agenda requests and supporting documents.\n\n2.  Prepare agenda, meeting notice and document package.\n\n3.  Coordinate with the Chairperson on scheduling and meeting requirements.\n\n4.  Record attendance, proceedings, resolutions and decisions.\n\n5.  Prepare draft minutes and resolutions for review and approval.\n\n6.  Distribute approved records to authorised members.\n\n7.  Enter decisions into the action register and assign responsible offices.\n\n8.  Track implementation and preserve the official records.\n\n**Required records**: Meeting calendar, agenda, attendance, minutes, resolutions, action register and document distribution log.\n\n','Loaded from reference/moo/consolidated/01_Chapter_1.md (split from MOO_Consolidated_v1.0.md, 12 Nov 2026)','draft')
